+     * shallow rule exactly as {@link #best} and the margin rule give it: its pick
+     * overrules Forge's answer when its shallow difference is above {@code margin} and
+     * it was not VETOED.  After a veto the play goes to Forge's answer or a promotion,
+     * never to the vetoed pick's runner-up: the runner-up has no deep evidence of its
+     * own (a settled one was retired by the race the round it settled; an unsettled one
+     * was never deepened) and usually shares the pick's flaw, and Forge's answer is the
+     * baseline the whole search is measured against.  Only where the shallow rule keeps
+     * Forge's answer, or after a veto, may a promotion act: the promoted alternative with
+     * the highest deep mean (the lower index on a tie), whether or not the race retired
+     * it at its own horizon.  So a vetoed alternative is never played, a promotion never
+     * displaces a shallow winner depth did not contradict, and with no veto and no
+     * promotion this is the shallow rule's verdict on the same shallow evidence.
+     */
+    public static int deepChoice(final boolean[] active, final List<double[]> leaves, final double[] diff, final int rounds,
+                                 final int margin, final int[] verdict, final double[] deepMean) {
+        final int best = best(active, leaves, diff, rounds);
+        if (best > 0 && diff[best] > margin && verdict[best] != DEEP_VETO) {
+            return best;
+        }
+        int promo = 0;
+        for (int i = 1; i < verdict.length; i++) {
+            if (verdict[i] == DEEP_PROMOTE && (promo == 0 || deepMean[i] > deepMean[promo])) {
+                promo = i;
+            }
+        }
+        return promo;
+    }
